module bindings.python.django_optional_password_signup;

// A password form field that the code itself makes optional still cleans to
// the empty string, and in Django's documented semantics empty and absent are
// different credentials: `AbstractBaseUser.set_password(raw_password)` hashes
// whatever string it is given, and only a null password -- `set_password(None)`
// or `create_user(..., password=None)` -- installs an unusable password that
// no login attempt can match. A signup flow that relaxes a form field's
// `Field.required` at runtime for an SSO or invitation path and then hands
// `cleaned_data['password']` straight to account creation registers the SSO
// user with a usable empty password, and the email-password backend sharing
// the realm accepts the empty string as that user's correct credential: one
// unauthenticated request to the login endpoint then yields the account and
// its API key.
//
// The shape is the conjunction, in one function's scope: the runtime
// relaxation (`...required = False` assigned on a bound form field or a
// `fields[...]` entry -- the assignment token renders the subscript key away,
// so the relaxation conjunct cannot and does not name the field; the
// password anchor is the consumption, and a class-level `required=False`
// declaration lives outside the function and is a separate residual), the
// consumption of the cleaned password value, and a password-bearing creation
// call (`set_password`, `create_user` -- the documented Django surface; a
// project wrapper whose name contains `create_user` resolves through the
// same token). Optional alone is harmless, consumed alone is a required
// field's normal signup; the account with a usable empty password exists
// only when both hold.
//
// Two spellings of the counterweight decline the flag, both read from the
// library's documentation rather than from the code under review: consulting
// the field's required-ness at the point of consumption -- a condition whose
// final conjunct reads `...field.required`, carried by a distinct token from
// the relaxation assignment's own text -- and coalescing the cleaned value to
// null (`... or None`), the documented way absence becomes an unusable
// password rather than an empty one.
//
// Residuals, stated rather than hidden. A truthiness guard on the value
// (`if not password: password = None`) and a bare condition reading
// `field.required` emit no boolean-operator token and do not decline, so
// those correct spellings are flagged. The required-ness negation is
// satisfied module-wide, so a sibling function in the same file consulting a
// field's required-ness suppresses this one. The `.get('password')`
// consumption spelling and numbered field names beyond `password` and
// `password1` are outside the needle set. And the function context keeps its
// first 512 tokens, so a flow long enough to drop the creation call from its
// context is not judged.
binding djangoOptionalPasswordEmptyCredential {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "python"
    and containsAny(node.context.assign, ["field.required=False", "].required=False"])
    and containsAny(node.context.subscript, [
      "cleaned_data['password']", "cleaned_data[\"password\"]",
      "cleaned_data['password1']", "cleaned_data[\"password1\"]"])
    and containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["set_password", "create_user"])
    and not (node.context.expr endsWith ".field.required")
    and not containsAny(node.context.assign, ["orNone"])
  emit issue code.EmptyPasswordAccountCreation at node
}
